  "question": {
    "text": "The correct group of halide ions which can be oxidised by oxygen in acidic medium is"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Br</mi><mo>&#8211;</mo></msup></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>&#8211;</mo></msup></math> only",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Br</mi><mo>&#8211;</mo></msup></math> only",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>&#8211;</mo></msup></math> only",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Cl</mi><mo>&#8211;</mo></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>Br</mi><mo>&#8211;</mo></msup></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>&#8211;</mo></msup></math> only",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Fluorine oxidises water to oxygen whereas chlorine &amp; bromine react with water to form corresponding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>HX</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>HOX</mi></math> acids.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mover><mo>&#8594;</mo><mrow/></mover><mn>4</mn><mi>HF</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"/><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mover><mo>&#8594;</mo><mrow/></mover><mi>HCl</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>HOCl</mi></math></p><p>The reaction of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;with water is nonspontaneous. In fact <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>&#8211;</mo></msup></math> can be oxidised by oxygen in acidic medium. It is why the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>&#8211;</mo></msup></math> in nature is not present in that much amount as other halides are present in nature.</p>"
  }
